
export anchor RibbonSPlanningTabOpensAPinnedPlanning2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        Opening the ribbon's Planning tab, by clicking it, with Alt+2, or by Ctrl+clicking
        it to open it alongside other tabs, opens the body's Planning tab and selects it,
        or selects it if it is already open. Today only the Open Planning button does this,
        as spec/ui/components/ribbon/planning-tab/index.pi#RibbonPlanningTab.openPlanning
        says. Read spec/ui/components/ribbon/index.pi#Ribbon.keyboard and
        spec/ui/components/ribbon/index.pi#Ribbon.openingSeveralTabs for how ribbon tabs open.

        Once open, the body's Planning tab can't be closed. It has no close button, and
        nothing else closes it either, the same as the Chat tab described in
        spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.tabs. Update
        spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.contents, which says the tab is there only
        "while it is open", and spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.description, which
        says Planning opens "as a file opens", to match. Planning sits right after Chat
        and can't be dragged, and file and task tabs can't be dropped in front of it.

        The spec says nothing yet about what the body shows when the ribbon switches from
        Planning to another tab, so this part is new. The Planning tab stays in the bar and
        the body's selection doesn't change. The Open Planning, New Story, Draft Stories and
        Research commands keep working as they do now.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            Opening the ribbon's Planning tab, by clicking it, with Alt+2, or by Ctrl+clicking
            it to open it alongside other tabs, opens the body's Planning tab and selects it,
            or selects it if it is already open. Today only the Open Planning button does this,
            as spec/ui/components/ribbon/planning-tab/index.pi#RibbonPlanningTab.openPlanning
            says. Read spec/ui/components/ribbon/index.pi#Ribbon.keyboard and
            spec/ui/components/ribbon/index.pi#Ribbon.openingSeveralTabs for how ribbon tabs open.

            Once open, the body's Planning tab can't be closed. It has no close button, and
            nothing else closes it either, the same as the Chat tab described in
            spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.tabs. Update
            spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.contents, which says the tab is there only
            "while it is open", and spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.description, which
            says Planning opens "as a file opens", to match. Planning sits right after Chat
            and can't be dragged, and file and task tabs can't be dropped in front of it.

            The spec says nothing yet about what the body shows when the ribbon switches from
            Planning to another tab, so this part is new. The Planning tab stays in the bar and
            the body's selection doesn't change. The Open Planning, New Story, Draft Stories and
            Research commands keep working as they do now.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I've updated the spec so that opening the ribbon's Planning tab opens the body's Planning tab and selects it, or just selects it if it's already open. This works by clicking the tab, with Alt+2, or with Ctrl+click alongside other tabs. `piton check` and `piton build` both ran without errors.

            Anchors the code should follow:

            - **`RibbonPlanningTab.description`**: all three ways of opening the ribbon tab go through the same route as Open Planning. Ctrl+clicking it while it's already open only closes the ribbon tab and opens nothing. Switching the ribbon from Planning to another tab, or closing Planning among several open tabs, leaves the body alone: the Planning tab stays in the bar and the selection doesn't change.
            - **`Body.tabs`**:
              - Once opened, Planning sits right after Chat and is pinned like Chat: no close button, nothing closes it, and it can't be dragged.
              - No file or task tab can be dropped in front of Chat or Planning.
              - A new file tab opened while Chat is selected goes just after Planning, never between the two.
            - **`Body.openFile`**: closing the last file now selects Planning if its tab is there, otherwise Chat. You didn't ask for this or the new-file placement above; I added both so new tabs and the selection never end up between Chat and Planning.
            - **`Body.contents`** and **`Planning.description`** now say the tab is pinned after Chat and stays once opened, instead of "while it is open" and "as a file opens".

            Open Planning, New Story, Draft Stories and Research are unchanged.
            \\\

    sentFrom: RibbonSPlanningTabOpensAPinnedPlanning

    model: opus
